{"name":"Sugar Coated","hex":"#ffedf1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugar coated","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative form of sugarcoated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugar_coated"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugar Coated","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar Coated is a Canadian documentary film, directed by Michèle Hozer and released in 2015. The film explores the impact on human health of the heavy use of sugar in the contemporary diet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0b/Sugar_Coated_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_Coated"},{"title":"Sugar","blocks":[{"text":"Sugar is a class of sweet-tasting, soluble carbohydrates, many of which are used in food. Simple sugars, also called monosaccharides, include glucose, fructose and galactose. Compound sugars, also called disaccharides or double sugars, are molecules made of two bonded monosaccharides; common examples are sucrose, lactose and maltose. White sugar is almost pure sucrose. During digestion, compound sugars are hydrolysed into simple sugars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg/330px-Sucre_blanc_cassonade_complet_rapadura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar"},{"title":"Coating","blocks":[{"text":"A coating is a covering that is applied to the surface of an object, or substrate. The purpose of applying the coating may be decorative, functional, or both. Coatings may be applied as liquids, gases or solids, e.g., powder coatings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Spraying_lacquer_on_cabinets_LCCN2016850642.jpg/330px-Spraying_lacquer_on_cabinets_LCCN2016850642.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coating"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/1280px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg/500px-Canaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugar cane plantation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanaviais_Sao_Paulo_01_2008_06.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/1280px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/500px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sugars (e.g. glucose) and their polymers (such as starch, cellulose).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Misri.JPG/500px-Misri.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Misri.JPG/1280px-Misri.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thu